type: software
title: "Ecological Detectability Boundaries in Nationwide Administrative Data: Replication Code and Prefecture-Level Dataset (NDB Open Data, Japan)"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-06-29
license: MIT
url: "https://github.com/haruki00430/ndb-pm25-diabetes-japan"
repository-code: "https://github.com/haruki00430/ndb-pm25-diabetes-japan"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21031754
abstract: >-
  Reproducible code and prefecture-level aggregated data (N = 47) for an
  ecological study examining ambient PM2.5 exposure and diabetes-related
  indicators across Japan's 47 prefectures using NDB Open Data (10th edition,
  FY2022 health checkups, FY2023 medical claims) and national air-quality
  monitoring data (FY2022–2023). The study introduces the concept of an
  ecological detectability boundary—a confluence of six characteristics that
  can jointly prevent detection of an established environmental effect in
  nationwide administrative data. PM2.5 was not significantly associated with
  prefecture-level mean HbA1c (β = 0.00371, p = 0.487) or diabetes medication
  prescriptions (β = 6884, p = 0.719) across all sensitivity analyses.
